      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment of an OIML certificate R51/2006-GB1-09.04, Revision 2. This is part 2 of 2, a fragment covering pages 4-7. It's a checkweigher certificate (R51 - Automatic catchweighing instruments).\n\nKey content:\n\n1. First table (page 4) — appears to be for Versa GP / Versa Rx models (given context \"The models Versa RxM and Versa Chain use this construction with the Slack Conveyor weighing system\"). Three model variants:\n   - Max capacity: 3000 g, 6000 g, 15000 g\n   - Min capacity: 100 g, 200 g, 500 g\n   - Scale interval e: ≥ 1 g, ≥ 2 g, ≥ 5 g\n   - Load cell Emax: 20 kg, 35 kg, 100 kg\n\n   Note: \"Other Emax values may be chosen provided the sensitivity is greater than 1 µV/e.\"\n\n2. Second table (page 4):\n   - Maximum number of scale intervals (n): 3000\n   - Tare (T): complex conditions\n   - Climatic environment: 0°C to +40°C, Non-condensing (closed)\n   - Electromagnetic environments: E1 and E2\n   - Power supply: 115 - 230 V a.c. 50/60 Hz\n   - Accuracy class: XIII (with footnote (1))\n\n3. Third table (page 4): Number of scale intervals vs Maximum conveyor speed:\n   - Min-100: 1.6 m/s\n   - 100–2000: 2.0 m/s\n   - 2000-3000: 1.6 m/s\n\n4. Software section: V312 Version 2.0, versions V312 2.0 (37.00.02.xx) standard, and dedicated versions 39.00.01.xx, 41.00.01.xx, 42.00.01.xx.\n\n5. Software modules table: MIDModule.dll 100.0.2.0 4000DE8A, DataProcessing.dll 32.0.2.0 4000DE8A, VW_Controls.dll 35.0.2.0 4000DE8A.\n\n6. Teorema section: conveyor description, load cell Tedea Huntleigh 1130 C3. Load cell conditions list.\n\n7. Technical characteristics for Teorema (page 6):\n   - Max capacity: 800 g, 1600 g, 3200 g\n   - Min capacity: 42 g, 84 g, 168 g\n   - Scale interval: multi-range values per model\n   - Load cell Emax: 30 kg, 50 kg, 100 kg\n   - \"Other Emax values may be chosen provided the sensitivity is greater than 0.33 µV/div.\"\n\n8. Teorema characteristics table (page 6):\n   - n: 1600\n   - Tare: T ≤ 30% Gross\n   - Climatic environment: 5°C to +40°C, non-condensing\n   - Electromagnetic: E1 and E2\n   - Power supply: 230 V a.c. 50/60 Hz\n   - Accuracy class: XIII(1)\n\n9. Weighing range vs max conveyor speed: W1: 1.6 m/s, W2: 2.6 m/s, W3: 2.6 m/s\n\n10. Automatic zero-setting frequency: 7 min 25 s.\n\n11. Certificate history table:\n    - R51/2006-GB1-09.04, 22 July 2009, Certificate first issued.\n    - revision 1, 25 January 2012, Versa GP, Versa RxC and Versa RxM models added. Software options added.\n    - revision 2, 28 July 2014, Software section added, etc.\n\nAlso mentioned models: Versa RxC, Versa RxM, Versa Chain, VersaWeigh, Versa GP, Teorema.\n\nNow, since this is a fragment, I should extract what's present:\n- characteristics (type_level and model_level)\n- model_family models (VersaWeigh, Versa GP, Versa RxC, Versa RxM, Versa Chain, Teorema)\n- matrix_tables (conveyor speed tables, software modules table, certificate history)\n- revision_history\n- recommendation: R51 — can I infer? The certificate number is R51/2006-GB1-09.04. The recommendation is OIML R51 (2006 edition?). The fragment doesn't explicitly state \"OIML R 51\" as the recommendation except the cert number. Hmm — should I include recommendation? The rules say omit sections not present. The certificate number references R51/2006. I could include recommendation with id \"R51\", edition 2006. That seems reasonable but it's derived. The instruction: \"Don't fabricate. If a field is not present in the markdown, omit it.\" The cert number clearly indicates R51 2006. I think including recommendation with id R51, edition 2006 is defensible but the fragment might be part of the certificate where the recommendation is stated in part 1. Since this is a fragment task, \"for the content present in THIS fragment only\". The recommendation R51 is referenced in the certificate number. I'll include it cautiously — accuracy class XIII is present. R51 scheme... not explicitly stated. Hmm. I'd lean to include recommendation: {\"id\": \"R51\", \"edition\": 2006, \"accuracy_classes\": [\"X\"]} — wait accuracy class XIII? Actually in OIML R51, accuracy classes are XI, XIII (i.e., X(1), X(2)? no). R51 accuracy classes are X, XI, XIII? Let me recall: OIML R51 automatic catchweighing instruments have accuracy classes XI and XIII? Actually classes are XI, XIII... Hmm, R51 defines accuracy classes XI, XIII — no wait. R51 classes: XI (special?), ... Let me think. OIML R 51: accuracy classes XI and XIII? No — it's \"XI\" and \"XIII\"? The classes are XI, XIII? Actually I recall R51 has accuracy classes XI and XIII. Hmm no. R51 (Automatic catchweighing instruments): accuracy classes XI, XIII — I believe classes are XI and XIII, yes. But the table says \"XIII(1)\" where (1) is a footnote marker. But the footnote definition is not in this fragment (it's probably in part 1). So I'd record accuracy class as \"XIII\" with footnote marker \"1)\"? The markdown shows \"XIII(1)\". The footnote (1) definition isn't in the fragment. Rule 7 says extract footnote definitions into footnotes[]; since no definitions present here, omit footnotes[].\n\nAccuracy classes: \"XIII\" — I'll record in characteristics type_level accuracy_class: value \"XIII\", footnote_markers [\"1)\"] for the first (Versa) set. But wait — there are two sets of characteristics: one for Versa (3000/6000/15000 g) and one for Teorema (800/1600/3200 g). These are different instrument families within the cert. Type-level attributes like climatic environment differ (0°C to +40°C vs 5°C to +40°C), power supply differs (115-230 V vs 230 V), n differs (3000 vs 1600). So these cannot all be single type_level values.\n\nHow to model? The cert covers two instrument families: Versa (with constructions VersaWeigh, Versa GP, Versa Rx [RxC, RxM], Versa Chain) and Teorema. Each family has 3 capacity variants.\n\nOption: use model_family models as family-variant combos and model_level entries per family. Or use config_level with axis \"instrument_family\". Or use matrix_tables to preserve the two tables and model_level with model ids like \"Versa 3000g\".\n\nGiven the schema, I think the cleanest approach:\n\nmodel_family:\n  family_name: null\n  models:\n    - VersaWeigh\n    - Versa GP\n    - Versa RxC\n    - Versa RxM\n    - Versa Chain\n    - Teorema\n\nActually the type designations: models mentioned: \"Versa RxC\", \"Versa RxM\", \"Versa Chain\", \"VersaWeigh\", \"Versa GP\", \"Teorema\". Software \"V312 Version 2.0\".\n\ncharacteristics.model_level: For the Versa table, the varying attribute is capacity variant. Perhaps define models as \"Versa (Max 3000 g)\", \"Versa (Max 6000 g)\", \"Versa (Max 15000 g)\" and \"Teorema (Max 800 g)\", etc. That's messy.\n\nAlternative: Use matrix_tables for each table, and model_level entries with model ids combining family and capacity. Let me design:\n\nmodels:\n- {\"model_id\": \"Versa Max=3000g\", \"label\": \"Versa family, 3000 g variant\"}\n- {\"model_id\": \"Versa Max=6000g\", ...}\n- {\"model_id\": \"Versa Max=15000g\", ...}\n- {\"model_id\": \"Teorema Max=800g\", ...}\n- {\"model_id\": \"Teorema Max=1600g\", ...}\n- {\"model_id\": \"Teorema Max=3200g\", ...}\n\nmodel_level entries:\n- maximum_capacity (g): Versa 3000/6000/15000; Teorema 800/1600/3200\n- minimum_capacity (g): 100/200/500; 42/84/168\n- scale_interval (g): Versa: \"≥1\", \"≥2\", \"≥5\" — value could be {\"min\": 1, \"max\": null}. Teorema: multi-range values (complex strings). For Teorema scale interval per model: \n  - 800g model: \"≥ 0.5 g (42 to 125 g); ≥ 1 g (125 to 250 g); ≥ 2 g (250 to 800 g)\" — multi-range.\n  I could record as string values, or as config-level. Perhaps record as value string. Hmm, rules say numbers not strings where possible; but multi-range interval lists are complex. I could represent value as an array? The schema says value: number | {min,max} | string. I'll use string for the multi-range ones.\n- load_cell_emax (kg): 20/35/100; 30/50/100\n\nType-level per family though: n (3000 vs 1600), climatic (0-40 vs 5-40), power supply, tare. These differ per family, not per capacity variant. Could use config_level with axis \"instrument family\": conditions \"Versa\", \"Teorema\". That fits config_level: attribute × config-axis matrix.\n\nconfig_level entries:\n- attribute: \"maximum_number_of_scale_intervals_nlc\" — actually here it's \"Maximum number of scale intervals (n)\" — canonical: max_scale_intervals_n. axis: \"instrument_family\", values: [{condition: \"Versa (VersaWeigh, Versa GP, Versa Rx, Versa Chain)\", value: 3000}, {condition: \"Teorema\", value: 1600}]\n- attribute: \"tare\", axis \"instrument_family\", values: strings for Versa complex conditions and \"≤ 30% Gross\" for Teorema. Value strings.\n- attribute: \"climatic_environment_temperature\", axis instrument_family: Versa {min 0, max 40} °C; Teorema {min 5, max 40} °C.\n- attribute: \"climatic_environment\", condition values \"0°C to +40°C\" + \"Non-condensing (closed)\". I'll do temperature as structured and humidity condition \"non-condensing (closed)\" as separate attribute.\n- attribute: \"electromagnetic_environment\": \"E1 and E2\" both.\n- attribute: \"power_supply_voltage\": Versa {min 115, max 230} V; Teorema 230 V. power_supply_frequency: {min 50, max 60} Hz both. power_supply_type \"AC\".\n- attribute: \"accuracy_class\": \"XIII\" with footnote marker 1) both.\n- attribute: \"automatic_zero_setting_frequency\": value \"7 min 25 s\" — could be structured: 7 min 25 s = 445 s. Keep as string \"7 min 25 s\" with unit? Or value 445 unit \"s\". Hmm. The rules: numbers not strings. \"7 min 25 s\" is a compound duration. I could record value 445, unit_symbol \"s\". But that transforms the data. Safer: value \"7 min 25 s\", unit_symbol null. Or value {min:7,max:25}? no. I'll do value: \"7 min 25 s\" — actually maybe unit_symbol \"min/s\"? No. Keep string.\n\nConveyor speed tables: these are config-level: attribute \"maximum_conveyor_speed\" with axis \"number_of_scale_intervals\" for Versa: conditions \"Min–100\": 1.6 m/s; \"100–2000\": 2.0; \"2000–3000\": 1.6. For Teorema, axis \"weighing_range\": W1 1.6, W2 2.6, W3 2.6.\n\nmatrix_tables: I could also capture these as matrix_tables. The schema says matrix_tables preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere. The conveyor speed tables fit config_level. The software modules table could be components? They're software modules with version and checksum — I could put in matrix_tables \"Legally relevant software modules\". Certificate history goes to revision_history.\n\nSoftware: I could add components[] entries for software? The schema components is for sub-assemblies. Software alternative V312 Version 2.0 — I can record as a component with role \"software\", type_designations [\"V312 Version 2.0\"], characteristics. That seems reasonable. Or matrix_tables. I'll add components with role \"legally_relevant_software\" and sub-table via characteristics or matrix_tables.\n\nLet me not overcomplicate. Plan:\n\n{\n  \"recommendation\": {\"id\": \"R51\", \"edition\": 2006},\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Checkweigher\" — category examples given include \"Measuring system\"... R51 instruments are \"Automatic catchweighing instruments\". I'll use \"Automatic catchweighing instrument (checkweigher)\" or just \"Checkweigher\". The fragment mentions \"Checkweigher (Static-Weigh) applications\". Category: \"Checkweigher\".\n    \"type_designations\": [\"VersaWeigh\", \"Versa GP\", \"Versa RxC\", \"Versa RxM\", \"Versa Chain\", \"Teorema\"]\n  },\n  \"model_family\": {\n    \"family_name\": \"Versa\",\n    \"models\": [...]\n  },\n\nHmm family_name: \"Versa\" is a family (VersaWeigh, Versa GP, Versa RxC, Versa RxM, Versa Chain). Teorema is a separate model. Maybe models list includes both families' variants. Since cert covers >1 model variant, model_family included. family_name: null (no explicit family name covering all). Models:\n\n- {\"model_id\": \"Versa 3000g\", \"label\": \"Versa family, Max = 3000 g variant\"}? Or better to combine family and capacity: \"VersaWeigh/Versa GP/Versa Rx (Max 3000 g)\". Hmm.\n\nActually the constructions: VersaWeigh, Versa GP, Versa Rx (RxC uses standard weighing system; RxM and Versa Chain use Slack Conveyor weighing system). And Teorema. The capacity tables: first table (page 4) follows the description of Versa constructions — Max 3000/6000/15000 g. Second characteristics set (page 6) is for Teorema — \"The instruments have the following technical characteristics\" following Teorema description: Max 800/1600/3200 g.\n\nSo the capacity variants are per family: Versa has 3 variants, Teorema has 3 variants.\n\nI'll define models with ids like \"Versa Max=3000g\", \"Versa Max=6000g\", \"Versa Max=15000g\", \"Teorema Max=800g\", \"Teorema Max=1600g\", \"Teorema Max=3200g\". Attributes include family.\n\nmodel_level:\n- maximum_capacity: unit g, values for the six models.\n- minimum_capacity: unit g.\n- scale_interval: unit g, values: Versa: {\"min\":1,\"max\":null}, {\"min\":2,\"max\":null}, {\"min\":5,\"max\":null}; Teorema: string multi-range. Let me write them as strings: \"≥ 0.5 g (42 to 125 g); ≥ 1 g (125 to 250 g); ≥ 2 g (250 to 800 g)\" — hmm but unit separation... These are multi-range, complex. Maybe better as config_level with axis \"weighing range\"? That's over-engineering. I'll represent scale_interval values as structured where possible: for Versa: value {\"min\": 1, \"max\": null} unit g. For Teorema: value as string \"0.5 g (42–125 g) / 1 g (125–250 g) / 2 g (250–800 g)\"? The rule says strings allowed for formulas/symbols. Multi-range lists — I think a string is acceptable, keeping unit_symbol null and embedding units. Or I could define scale_interval_minimum values: 0.5, 1, 2 g etc. with a note. I'll go with string values for Teorema (with unit \"g\" but strings contain ranges...). Hmm, mixing: for consistency, use value strings for all Teorema scale intervals, and structured {min} for Versa. unit_symbol \"g\" for all.\n\nActually cleaner: attribute \"minimum_scale_interval\" for Versa: values 1, 2, 5 g ({min:1,max:null}). For Teorema: multi-interval — record as string: \"≥0.5 (42–125 g); ≥1 (125–250 g); ≥2 (250–800 g)\". I'll keep unit_symbol null for the string ones to avoid confusion? The instructions say pure value+unit separation; for a string value containing units, unit_symbol null is fine.\n\n- load_cell_emax: unit kg, values 20/35/100 and 30/50/100. Also note \"Other Emax values may be chosen provided sensitivity > 1 µV/e (Versa) / > 0.33 µV/div (Teorema)\". Record as type_level? It's a condition; I could add to matrix description or as a type_level characteristic \"load_cell_alternatives_condition\": value \"Other Emax values may be chosen provided the sensitivity is greater than 1 µV/e\" — per family though. Could add config_level. I'll include config_level entries: attribute \"load_cell_emax_alternative_condition\", axis \"instrument_family\": values strings \"Sensitivity > 1 µV/e\" and \"Sensitivity > 0.33 µV/div\".\n\nconfig_level entries (axis \"instrument family\"):\n1. max_number_of_scale_intervals_n: Versa 3000, Teorema 1600 (dimensionless)\n2. tare: strings\n3. climatic_temperature_range: {min 0, max 40} °C vs {min 5, max 40} °C\n4. climatic_humidity_condition: \"Non-condensing (closed)\" both\n5. electromagnetic_environment: \"E1 and E2\" both\n6. power_supply_voltage: {115,230} V vs 230 V\n7. power_supply_frequency: {50,60} Hz vs {50,60} Hz\n8. power_supply_type: \"AC\"\n9. accuracy_class: \"XIII\" footnote marker \"1)\"\n\nconfig_level entries (axis \"number of scale intervals\" — Versa conveyor speed):\n- maximum_conveyor_speed: conditions \"Min–100\": 1.6 m/s; \"100–2000\": 2.0 m/s; \"2000–3000\": 1.6 m/s\n\nconfig_level (axis \"weighing range\" — Teorema):\n- maximum_conveyor_speed: W1: 1.6; W2: 2.6; W3: 2.6 m/s\n\nBut config_level schema: values[]: {condition, value, unit_symbol}. Can I have two different axes for same attribute? Yes, separate entries each with own axis.\n\nmatrix_tables: \n- \"Legally relevant software modules\": columns designation, version, checksum; rows 3.\n- Maybe \"Certificate history\" — goes to revision_history instead.\n- Conveyor speed tables → config_level as decided; but could also add matrix_tables. I'll keep them in config_level only... Actually maybe include as matrix_tables too for fidelity? The guidance: preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere. They fit in config_level. I'll keep config_level only to avoid duplication. Hmm, but duplication is allowed in the R137 example (both matrix_tables AND model_level). I'll include the speed tables as matrix_tables too? To be safe and faithful, I'll include them as matrix_tables named \"Maximum conveyor speed vs number of scale intervals (Versa)\" and \"Maximum conveyor speed vs weighing range (Teorema)\". And also config_level. Slight duplication is fine per example. Actually, I'll include them only in config_level to keep the output tight. Hmm... The R137 example explicitly duplicates. I'll duplicate — it's safer for downstream consumers.\n\nLet me decide: include both.\n\ncomponents: software modules. The cert designates software V312 Version 2.0 with modules. I'll add components:\n- {\"role\": \"legally_relevant_software\", \"type_designations\": [\"V312 Version 2.0\"], \"characteristics\": {...}} — characteristics in components is {} free-form. I can put version info: {\"standard_version_code\": \"37.00.02.xx\", \"dedicated_versions\": [...]}. Also modules table via matrix_tables.\n\nAlso load cell component: Tedea Huntleigh 1130 C3 (tested), with alternatives: any compatible R60 load cell meeting conditions. Components entry: {\"role\": \"load_cell\", \"type_designations\": [\"Tedea Huntleigh 1130 C3\"], \"alternatives\": \"OR\"? Actually the alternative is \"any compatible load cell providing conditions met\" — alternatives \"OR\" applies to named alternatives. I'll note characteristics: {\"tested_capacity\": \"30 kg\"}. And conditions as description? components schema: role, type_designations[], alternatives, characteristics{}. I'll include characteristics {\"capacity\": {\"value\":30,\"unit_symbol\":\"kg\"}} for the tested cell.\n\nAlso software modules as components each? Better: matrix_tables for module table.\n\nrevision_history:\n- revision \"0\": date 22 July 2009 (2009-07-22), changes \"Certificate first issued.\"\n- revision \"1\": 2012-01-25, \"Versa GP, Versa RxC and Versa RxM models added. Software options added.\"\n- revision \"2\": 2014-07-28, \"Software section added, software options section replaced by Alternative software section. Teorema instrument and Derived alternative sections added. Remote cabinets section added. Certificate history added.\"\n\nDates: use ISO format? Schema says date; I'll use ISO \"2009-07-22\" etc. That's a transformation but standard. The example schema shows date fields; ISO is safer.\n\nrecommendation: id \"R51\", edition 2006. accuracy_classes: R51 classes are XI and XIII? The cert only mentions XIII. Should I fill accuracy_classes with [\"XIII\"]? The rule for recommendation.accuracy_classes presumably from cert content. The fragment shows accuracy class XIII. I'll include [\"XIII\"]. Hmm, but the (1) footnote... class XIII is indeed an R51 class (X(1)?? no). R51 accuracy classes: XI and XIII. So XIII fine. But maybe leave accuracy_classes out since it's type-level characteristic? The recommendation schema includes accuracy_classes[] — I'll include [\"XIII\"].\n\nAlso certificate number etc. — not in fragment? Actually \"OIML Certificate No R51/2006-GB1-09.04 Revision 2\" appears in page headers! So certificate.number = \"R51/2006-GB1-09.04\", revision 2. The certificate object: number, scheme, project_number, page_total, member_state, date_issued, oiml_issuer_id. From fragment: number \"R51/2006-GB1-09.04\", page_total 7 (\"This certificate includes 7 pages\"). Revision 2 → revision_history covers. scheme? Not stated (GB1 suggests UK... actually \"GB1\" is the country code of GB — NMi? No, NMi is NL. GB1 = UK). member_state: not explicit; \"GB1\" indicates United Kingdom. I could infer member_state \"GB\"? Don't fabricate — but \"GB1\" in cert number strongly implies GB (United Kingdom). I'll omit member_state to be safe, or include? I'll omit. date_issued: not in fragment (revision 2 date 2014-07-28 could be issue date of this revision — the history says revision 2 dated 28 July 2014). Could set date_issued 2014-07-28? It's the date of revision 2. I'll omit to avoid overreach... Actually the revision history gives it; date_issued for the current revision = 2014-07-28 is reasonable. Hmm. I'll include date_issued \"2014-07-28\" — no wait, don't fabricate; the fragment doesn't say \"date of issue\". I'll omit.\n\ncertificate.page_total: 7. scheme: omit. project_number: omit.\n\nNow the tare conditions for Versa: \n\"T ≤ 30 % Gross for Gross ≤ 500e\nT ≤ 50% Gross for 500e < Gross ≤ 3000e for speed ≤ 1.8 m/s\nT ≤ 15% Gross for 500e < Gross ≤ 2000e for speed ≥ 1.8 m/s\nT ≤ 50% Gross for 2000e < Gross ≤ 3000e\n(with e1 replacing e for multi-range instruments)\"\n\nRecord as string value. Could break into config_level axis \"gross\" but messy — keep string.\n\nVersa scale interval values: \"≥ 1 g\", \"≥ 2 g\", \"≥ 5 g\" → {min:1,max:null} etc.\n\nTeorema scale interval strings:\nModel 800g: \"≥ 0.5 g (42 to 125 g); ≥ 1 g (125 to 250 g); ≥ 2 g (250 to 800 g)\"\nModel 1600g: \"≥ 1 g (84 to 250 g); ≥ 2 g (250 to 500 g); ≥ 5 g (500 to 1600 g)\"\nModel 3200g: \"≥ 2 g (168 to 500 g); ≥ 5 g (500 to 1000 g); ≥ 10 g (1000 to 3200 g)\"\n\nI'll store as strings.\n\nAutomatic zero-setting frequency 7 min 25 s — type_level? It's in the Teorema section (page 7 \"The frequency of automatic zero-setting is 7 min 25 s.\"). It appears after Teorema table, likely applying to Teorema. Put in config_level with condition Teorema. Or type_level. I'll put config_level axis instrument_family condition \"Teorema\", value \"7 min 25 s\" — or structured value 445 s? \"7 min 25 s\" = 445 s. I'll keep as string to preserve fidelity: {\"value\": \"7 min 25 s\", \"unit_symbol\": null}. Hmm the strict structured rule: numbers not strings... it's a compound; string acceptable.\n\nNow attribute naming: \n- \"maximum_capacity\" → canonical for R51 Max. Use \"maximum_capacity_max\"? Rules examples: maximum_capacity. I'll use \"maximum_capacity\".\n- \"minimum_capacity\" → \"minimum_capacity\".\n- \"scale_interval\" → \"scale_interval\" (values are minimum allowed; label \"scale_interval\").\n- \"load_cell_emax\" → \"load_cell_emax\".\n- \"maximum_number_of_scale_intervals\" → \"max_scale_intervals_n\".\n- \"maximum_conveyor_speed\".\n- \"tare\" → \"tare_limit\".\n- \"climatic_environment_temperature\" → I'll use \"temperature_range\" with unit °C. Canonical: \"temperature_range\".\n- \"humidity_condition\": \"non_condensing_closed\" or attribute \"climatic_environment_humidity\". I'll use \"environment_humidity_condition\": value \"Non-condensing (closed)\". Actually simpler: \"climatic_environment\": value \"Non-condensing (closed)\"? The table row \"Climatic environment\" has two cells: temp range and non-condensing. I'll create \"temperature_range\" and \"condensation\" → value \"non-condensing (closed)\". Use attribute \"climatic_environment_humidity\".\n- \"electromagnetic_environment\": value \"E1 and E2\" — or [\"E1\",\"E2\"]? value must be number|range|string → \"E1, E2\". I'll use string \"E1 and E2\".\n- \"power_supply_voltage\", \"power_supply_frequency\", \"power_supply_type\".\n- \"accuracy_class\": \"XIII\".\n- \"automatic_zero_setting_frequency\".\n- \"software_version\" as component characteristic.\n\nFootnote markers: \"XIII(1)\" → footnote_markers [\"1)\"]. Wait the markdown shows \"XIII(1)\". Marker format: earlier examples use \"1)\". I'll use [\"1)\"]. Since footnote definition not present, footnotes[] omitted.\n\nAlso sensitivity notes: \"sensitivity greater than 1 µV/e\" — unit \"µV/e\" → symbol \"µV/e\". Record config_level attribute \"load_cell_alternative_sensitivity_min\": value {\"min\":1,\"max\":null} unit \"µV/e\" condition Versa; {\"min\":0.33,\"max\":null} unit \"µV/div\" condition Teorema. Note Versa says µV/e and Teorema µV/div — keep as given.\n\nLoad cell conditions list (Teorema compatible load cell requirements): could be components characteristics or a description. I'll add components entry:\n\n{\n \"role\": \"load_cell\",\n \"type_designations\": [\"Tedea Huntleigh 1130 C3\"],\n \"characteristics\": {\n   \"tested_capacity\": {\"value\": 30, \"unit_symbol\": \"kg\"},\n   \"compatibility_conditions\": [...list of strings...]\n },\n \"alternatives\": \"OR\"\n}\n\nAlternatives \"OR\" — the tested cell OR any compatible load cell meeting conditions. I'll set alternatives: \"OR\".\n\nSoftware component:\n\n{\n \"role\": \"legally_relevant_software\",\n \"type_designations\": [\"V312 Version 2.0\"],\n \"characteristics\": {\n   \"standard_version\": \"V312 2.0 (37.00.02.xx)\",\n   \"dedicated_versions\": [\"Tare/Gross applications (39.00.01.xx)\", \"Multiproduct applications (41.00.01.xx)\", \"Twin application (42.00.01.xx)\"],\n   \"display_location\": \"Weigh menu 'Run Screen'\"\n }\n}\n\nSoftware modules table → matrix_tables.\n\nConveyor speed tables → matrix_tables + config_level.\n\nNow model_family models with attributes? Schema: models[]: model_id, label, attributes{}. I can embed per-model attributes into attributes{} too, but that duplicates model_level. The R60 example shows models without attributes. I'll include model_id and maybe label with family info, and attributes with family name:\n\nmodels:\n- {\"model_id\": \"Versa Max=3000g\", \"label\": \"Versa family (VersaWeigh, Versa GP, Versa Rx constructions), 3000 g variant\"}\n- etc.\n\nHmm, actually first table applies to which constructions? Context: page 4 fragment begins mid-description: \"Having a modified construction. The instrument is constructed in stainless steel... The model Versa RxC uses this construction with the standard weighing system. The models Versa RxM and Versa Chain use this construction with the Slack Conveyor weighing system.\" Then table. Then page 5-6 Teorema section. The derived alternatives says \"combination of the Teorema load cells in the above table and Weighing engine may be used in the VersaWeigh, Versa GP and Versa Rx constructions\". So the first characteristics set (3000/6000/15000 g) is for Versa (VersaWeigh, Versa GP, Versa Rx/RxC/RxM, Versa Chain). Good.\n\nSo model ids: maybe better to tie capacities to family: \"Versa-3000g\" etc. I'll use \"Versa (Max 3000 g)\". JSON keys fine.\n\nAlternatively define models as constructions and treat capacity as config axis. But capacities have paired Min and e values → capacity variant is the model axis. I'll go with 6 models.\n\nType designations list: [\"VersaWeigh\", \"Versa GP\", \"Versa RxC\", \"Versa RxM\", \"Versa Chain\", \"Teorema\"]. Also software \"V312\" is software not type. Load cell not a type of this cert.\n\nCategory: R51 = automatic catchweighing instruments. I'll use \"Checkweigher\".\n\nNow build model_level:\n\n1. maximum_capacity, unit \"g\":\n   Versa 3000/6000/15000; Teorema 800/1600/3200.\n2. minimum_capacity, unit \"g\": 100/200/500; 42/84/168.\n3. scale_interval, unit \"g\": Versa {min:1},{min:2},{min:5}; Teorema strings.\n4. load_cell_emax, unit \"kg\": 20/35/100; 30/50/100.\n\nconfig_level:\naxis \"instrument family\":\n- max_scale_intervals_n: Versa 3000 (unit null), Teorema 1600.\n- tare: strings.\n- temperature_range: {0,40} °C; {5,40} °C.\n- climatic_environment_humidity: \"Non-condensing (closed)\" both.\n- electromagnetic_environment: \"E1 and E2\" both.\n- power_supply_voltage: Versa {115,230} V; Teorema 230 V.\n- power_supply_frequency: {50,60} Hz both.\n- power_supply_type: \"AC\" both.\n- accuracy_class: \"XIII\" footnote [\"1)\"] both.\n- automatic_zero_setting_frequency: Teorema \"7 min 25 s\" (only Teorema condition; omit Versa).\n- load_cell_alternative_min_sensitivity: Versa {min:1,max:null} \"µV/e\"; Teorema {min:0.33,max:null} \"µV/div\".\n\naxis \"number of scale intervals\" (Versa):\n- maximum_conveyor_speed: conditions \"Min–100\" → 1.6 m/s; \"100–2000\" → 2.0; \"2000–3000\" → 1.6.\n\naxis \"weighing range\" (Teorema):\n- maximum_conveyor_speed: \"W1\" 1.6; \"W2\" 2.6; \"W3\" 2.6.\n\nmatrix_tables:\n1. \"Legally relevant software modules\": columns [\"designation\",\"version\",\"checksum\"], rows 3.\n2. \"Maximum conveyor speed by number of scale intervals (Versa)\": columns [\"number_of_scale_intervals\",\"maximum_conveyor_speed\"], rows.\n3. \"Maximum conveyor speed by weighing range (Teorema)\": same pattern.\n\nMaybe fold 2 & 3 into config_level only. I'll include them as matrix_tables AND config_level? I said duplicate. Eh — I'll include in config_level only for speeds, and matrix_tables only for software modules. Hmm, but the \"MOST IMPORTANT\" rule 4 says detect matrices; the speed tables are config matrices; config_level is the right place. The R137 example put the 2-D flow table in both. To be faithful, I'll duplicate speed tables into matrix_tables as well. It's low cost.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: duplication may cause downstream double counting. The example explicitly does it though. I'll include both, consistent with the example.\n\nrevision_history:\n[{\"revision\":\"0\",\"date\":\"2009-07-22\",\"changes\":\"Certificate first issued.\"},\n {\"revision\":\"1\",\"date\":\"2012-01-25\",\"changes\":\"Versa GP, Versa RxC and Versa RxM models added. Software options added.\"},\n {\"revision\":\"2\",\"date\":\"2014-07-28\",\"changes\":\"Software section added, software options section replaced by Alternative software section. Teorema instrument and Derived alternative sections added. Remote cabinets section added. Certificate history added.\"}]\n\ncertificate: {\"number\":\"R51/2006-GB1-09.04\",\"page_total\":7}. Also revision 2 — schema has revision_history; certificate object doesn't have revision field. OK.\n\nrecommendation: {\"id\":\"R51\",\"edition\":2006,\"accuracy_classes\":[\"X13\"?]} — accuracy class written \"XIII\". Hmm R51 classes: XI and XIII... wait, actually is it \"XIII\"? R51 accuracy classes are XI and XIII? Let me recall: OIML R51 classes: XI (accuracy class XI?) Hmm. R51 (Automatic catchweighing): classes XI and XIII? I think yes: class XI and class XIII (like R76's special classes are Ⅰ, Ⅱ → Ⅰ, Ⅱ, Ⅲ, ⅢI for non-automatic; for automatic weighers R51 uses XI and XIII). Yes, R51 defines accuracy classes XI and XIII. So \"XIII\" fine.\n\ncertified_type.description: brief description from fragment? There's construction description: \"constructed in stainless steel and part of an integrated self-carrying cabinet on adjustable feet, with a see through door\" for modified construction; Teorema conveyor description. I could add a short description. Keep minimal: description combining: \"Checkweigher constructions VersaWeigh, Versa GP, Versa Rx (standard or Slack Conveyor weighing system) and Teorema with remote cabinet; stainless steel construction...\" I'll include a modest description.\n\nComponents: also \"weighing engine\" and \"HMI\" mentioned: \"The software is held in the Weigh Engine and partially on the HMI\". Components could include: Weigh Engine, HMI, remote cabinet. I'll add:\n\ncomponents:\n1. load_cell (Tedea Huntleigh 1130 C3, alternatives OR compatible).\n2. legally_relevant_software (V312 Version 2.0).\n3. Maybe \"remote_control_cabinet\" — mentions \"The remote control cabinet houses the electrical hardware... including the control and display unit (HMI).\" And \"Remote cabinets: Having the VersaWeigh and Versa GP instruments fitted with remote cabinets, provided the load cell is connected to the cabinet using a metal conduit.\" I'll add component role \"remote_cabinet\" type_designations null? type_designations[] — no designations given; omit or empty. I'll include with characteristics {\"load_cell_connection\": \"metal conduit\"}. Hmm, maybe skip to avoid clutter. I'll include it briefly — it's a named sub-assembly. I'll include.\n\nFootnotes: none defined → omit.\n\nNow write the JSON. Ensure numbers, no LaTeX, snake_case keys.\n\nScale interval Versa: value {\"min\": 1, \"max\": null}? \"≥ 1 g\" → {min:1,max:null}. Good.\n\nTeorema scale interval strings — include as value strings, unit_symbol null (units embedded). E.g. \"≥ 0.5 g (42–125 g); ≥ 1 g (125–250 g); ≥ 2 g (250–800 g)\". Use original \"to\". I'll write \"≥0.5 g (42 to 125 g); ≥1 g (125 to 250 g); ≥2 g (250 to 800 g)\".\n\nTare Versa string: \"T ≤ 30% Gross for Gross ≤ 500e; T ≤ 50% Gross for 500e < Gross ≤ 3000e for speed ≤ 1.8 m/s; T ≤ 15% Gross for 500e < Gross ≤ 2000e for speed ≥ 1.8 m/s; T ≤ 50% Gross for 2000e < Gross ≤ 3000e (with e1 replacing e for multi-range instruments)\".\n\nTeorema tare: \"T ≤ 30% Gross\" → could be structured value {min:null,max:30} unit \"%\"? value {max:30} with reference \"Gross\". Rule 3 example: \"150% of Emax\" → value 150 unit \"%\" plus separate reference characteristic. For tare, I'll do attribute \"tare_limit\": value {\"min\": null, \"max\": 30}, unit_symbol \"%\", plus \"tare_reference\": \"Gross\"? That's noisy; simpler keep string \"T ≤ 30% Gross\". Hmm. I'll use structured for Teorema: value {min:null,max:30}, unit \"%\", and mention reference in a parallel attribute? I'll keep strings for both to preserve the conditional Versa text; for Teorema string \"≤ 30% of Gross\". Consistency: strings.\n\nActually rule 2 says structured values STRICT for every characteristic value, with strings allowed for formulas/symbols. Tare conditions are conditional statements — string OK.\n\nautomatic_zero_setting_frequency: \"7 min 25 s\". Keep string. Or value 445 unit \"s\"? I'll keep \"7 min 25 s\".\n\nPower supply: \"115 - 230 Va.c. 50/60 Hz\" → voltage {min:115,max:230} V, frequency {min:50,max:60} Hz, type AC. Teorema \"230 Va.c. 50/60 Hz\" → 230 V.\n\nClimatic: \"0°C to +40 °C\" → {min:0,max:40} °C. \"5°C to +40 °C\" → {min:5,max:40} °C.\n\nModel IDs: \"Versa Max=3000g\" — I'll use format from example: \"Emax=25t\". So \"Max=3000g\". But need family distinction: \"Versa Max=3000g\" and \"Teorema Max=800g\". Good.\n\nNow model_level entries use these model ids.\n\nLet me also add label per model: e.g. label \"Versa family – 3000 g capacity variant\". And attributes {\"family\": \"Versa\"}. Schema allows attributes{}.\n\nmatrix_tables column_units: schema example includes column_units. Schema in yaml lists columns[], rows[], description, footnotes[] — the R137 example adds column_units. I'll include column_units where applicable.\n\nSpeed tables columns: [\"number_of_scale_intervals\",\"maximum_conveyor_speed\"], column_units [null, \"m/s\"]. Rows:\n- {\"number_of_scale_intervals\":\"Min – 100\",\"maximum_conveyor_speed\":1.6}\n- {\"number_of_scale_intervals\":\"100 – 2000\",\"maximum_conveyor_speed\":2.0}\n- {\"number_of_scale_intervals\":\"2000 – 3000\",\"maximum_conveyor_speed\":1.6}\n\nTeorema: {\"weighing_range\":\"W1\",\"maximum_conveyor_speed\":1.6}, W2 2.6, W3 2.6.\n\nSoftware modules table: columns [\"designation\",\"version_number\",\"checksum\"]; rows:\n{\"designation\":\"MIDModule.dll\",\"version_number\":\"100.0.2.0\",\"checksum\":\"4000DE8A\"}\n{\"designation\":\"DataProcessing.dll\",\"version_number\":\"32.0.2.0\",\"checksum\":\"4000DE8A\"}\n{\"designation\":\"VW_Controls.dll\",\"version_number\":\"35.0.2.0\",\"checksum\":\"4000DE8A\"}\n\nconfig_level structure: entries: {\"attribute\": ..., \"axis\": ..., \"values\": [{\"condition\": ..., \"value\": ..., \"unit_symbol\": ..., \"footnote_markers\": [...]}]}.\n\nNow assemble full JSON.\n\nAlso type_level: is there anything constant across everything? Electromagnetic environment E1 and E2 is same for both; humidity non-condensing same; accuracy class XIII same; power frequency same. I could put those in type_level and family-varying in config_level. That reduces duplication. Let's do:\n\ntype_level:\n- electromagnetic_environment: \"E1 and E2\"\n- climatic_environment_humidity: \"Non-condensing (closed)\"\n- power_supply_frequency: {min:50,max:60} Hz\n- power_supply_type: \"AC\"\n- accuracy_class: \"XIII\" footnote [\"1)\"]\n\nconfig_level (axis instrument family):\n- maximum_capacity? No — that's model_level.\n- temperature_range: Versa {0,40}°C, Teorema {5,40}°C\n- power_supply_voltage: {115,230} V vs 230 V\n- max_scale_intervals_n: 3000 vs 1600\n- tare: strings\n- automatic_zero_setting_frequency: Teorema only\n- load_cell_alternative_min_sensitivity: µV/e 1 vs µV/div 0.33\n\nHmm, but splitting humidity/frequency to type_level while voltage to config_level... acceptable. Actually maybe keep ALL family-varying table rows in config_level (including duplicates) for table fidelity? Duplication again. I'll go with the split: constants → type_level; varying → config_level. This is cleaner and matches schema intent.\n\naccuracy_class at type_level: value \"XIII\", footnote_markers [\"1)\"].\n\nIs accuracy_class also in recommendation.accuracy_classes? Yes [\"XIII\"].\n\nLet me write out models:\n\n\"models\": [\n  {\"model_id\": \"Versa Max=3000g\", \"label\": \"Versa family (VersaWeigh / Versa GP / Versa Rx) 3000 g variant\", \"attributes\": {\"family\": \"Versa\"}},\n  {\"model_id\": \"Versa Max=6000g\", ...},\n  {\"model_id\": \"Versa Max=15000g\", ...},\n  {\"model_id\": \"Teorema Max=800g\", \"label\": \"Teorema 800 g variant\", \"attributes\": {\"family\": \"Teorema\"}},\n  {\"model_id\": \"Teorema Max=1600g\", ...},\n  {\"model_id\": \"Teorema Max=3200g\", ...}\n]\n\nfamily_name: null.\n\nmodel_level:\n1. {\"attribute\":\"maximum_capacity\",\"unit_symbol\":\"g\",\"values\":[6 entries]}\n2. {\"attribute\":\"minimum_capacity\",\"unit_symbol\":\"g\",\"values\":[6]}\n3. {\"attribute\":\"scale_interval\",\"unit_symbol\":\"g\",\"values\":[Versa three {min...}; Teorema three strings with unit_symbol null? Values array items have {model, value, footnote_markers}. Schema model_level values: {model, value, footnote_markers} — unit at attribute level. For Teorema strings with embedded units, unit_symbol \"g\" at attribute level still roughly applies (each embedded value has g). OK set unit_symbol \"g\" for the attribute; string values include ranges. Fine.\n4. {\"attribute\":\"load_cell_emax\",\"unit_symbol\":\"kg\",\"values\":[6]}\n\nconfig_level entries:\n\n{\"attribute\":\"maximum_number_of_scale_intervals\",\"axis\":\"instrument family\",\"values\":[{\"condition\":\"Versa (VersaWeigh, Versa GP, Versa Rx, Versa Chain)\",\"value\":3000},{\"condition\":\"Teorema\",\"value\":1600}]}\n\ncanonical label: \"max_scale_intervals_n\" per example rule (Max. number of load cell intervals nLC → max_load_cell_intervals_nlc). Here it's scale intervals n → \"max_scale_intervals_n\".\n\n{\"attribute\":\"tare\",\"axis\":\"instrument family\",\"values\":[{\"condition\":\"Versa...\",\"value\":\"T ≤ 30% Gross for Gross ≤ 500e; T ≤ 50% Gross for 500e < Gross ≤ 3000e for speed ≤ 1.8 m/s; T ≤ 15% Gross for 500e < Gross ≤ 2000e for speed ≥ 1.8 m/s; T ≤ 50% Gross for 2000e < Gross ≤ 3000e (with e1 replacing e for multi-range instruments)\"},{\"condition\":\"Teorema\",\"value\":\"T ≤ 30% Gross\"}]}\n\n{\"attribute\":\"temperature_range\",\"axis\":\"instrument family\",\"values\":[{\"condition\":\"Versa...\",\"value\":{\"min\":0,\"max\":40},\"unit_symbol\":\"°C\"},{\"condition\":\"Teorema\",\"value\":{\"min\":5,\"max\":40},\"unit_symbol\":\"°C\"}]}\n\n{\"attribute\":\"power_supply_voltage\",\"axis\":\"instrument family\",\"values\":[{\"condition\":\"Versa...\",\"value\":{\"min\":115,\"max\":230},\"unit_symbol\":\"V\"},{\"condition\":\"Teorema\",\"value\":230,\"unit_symbol\":\"V\"}]}\n\n{\"attribute\":\"load_cell_alternative_min_sensitivity\",\"axis\":\"instrument family\",\"values\":[{\"condition\":\"Versa (other Emax values may be chosen)\",\"value\":{\"min\":1,\"max\":null},\"unit_symbol\":\"µV/e\"},{\"condition\":\"Teorema (other Emax values may be chosen)\",\"value\":{\"min\":0.33,\"max\":null},\"unit_symbol\":\"µV/div\"}]}\n\n{\"attribute\":\"automatic_zero_setting_frequency\",\"axis\":\"instrument family\",\"values\":[{\"condition\":\"Teorema\",\"value\":\"7 min 25 s\"}]}\n\n{\"attribute\":\"maximum_conveyor_speed\",\"axis\":\"number of scale intervals (Versa)\",\"values\":[{\"condition\":\"Min – 100\",\"value\":1.6,\"unit_symbol\":\"m/s\"},{\"condition\":\"100 – 2000\",\"value\":2.0,\"unit_symbol\":\"m/s\"},{\"condition\":\"2000 – 3000\",\"value\":1.6,\"unit_symbol\":\"m/s\"}]}\n\n{\"attribute\":\"maximum_conveyor_speed\",\"axis\":\"weighing range (Teorema)\",\"values\":[{\"condition\":\"W1\",\"value\":1.6,\"unit_symbol\":\"m/s\"},{\"condition\":\"W2\",\"value\":2.6,\"unit_symbol\":\"m/s\"},{\"condition\":\"W3\",\"value\":2.6,\"unit_symbol\":\"m/s\"}]}\n\ntype_level:\n{\"electromagnetic_environment\":{\"value\":\"E1 and E2\",\"unit_symbol\":null}}\n{\"climatic_environment_humidity\":{\"value\":\"Non-condensing (closed)\",\"unit_symbol\":null}}\n{\"power_supply_frequency\":{\"value\":{\"min\":50,\"max\":60},\"unit_symbol\":\"Hz\"}}\n{\"power_supply_type\":{\"value\":\"AC\"}}\n{\"accuracy_class\":{\"value\":\"XIII\",\"unit_symbol\":null,\"footnote_markers\":[\"1)\"]}}\n\nNote: structured values shape requires value/unit_symbol/unit_id/footnote_markers. I'll include unit_id: null everywhere? The example shapes show unit_symbol and footnote_markers; unit_id \"Leave null unless certain\". I'll include unit_id: null in entries. That's verbose but per spec.\n\nComponents:\n\n[\n {\"role\":\"load_cell\",\"type_designations\":[\"Tedea Huntleigh 1130 C3\"],\"alternatives\":\"OR\",\"characteristics\":{\n   \"tested_capacity\":{\"value\":30,\"unit_symbol\":\"kg\",\"unit_id\":null},\n   \"compatibility_conditions\":[\n     \"Must have an OIML R60 Certificate of Conformity containing the load cell types and data required for the manufacturer's declaration of compatibility of modules\",\n     \"A load cell marked NH allowed only if humidity testing to R76 has been conducted\",\n     \"Not a load cell with digital output\",\n     \"Characteristics nlc, Y, Z same or better than the dynamically tested load cell (Tedea Huntleigh 1130 C3, capacity 30 kg)\",\n     \"Same design and material\",\n     \"No oil damper used\"\n   ]\n }},\n {\"role\":\"legally_relevant_software\",\"type_designations\":[\"V312 Version 2.0\"],\"characteristics\":{\n   \"standard_version\":\"V312 2.0 (37.00.02.xx)\",\n   \"standard_version_options\":[\"Multi-Lane applications\",\"Intermittent Checkweigher (Static-Weigh) applications (restricted to Slack Conveyor weighing systems and maximum capacities ≤ 1500 g)\",\"Data Matrix applications\",\"OEM applications\",\"Teorema applications\"],\n   \"dedicated_versions\":[{\"application\":\"Tare/Gross\",\"version_code\":\"39.00.01.xx\"},{\"application\":\"Multiproduct\",\"version_code\":\"41.00.01.xx\"},{\"application\":\"Twin\",\"version_code\":\"42.00.01.xx\"}],\n   \"version_display\":\"Weigh menu 'Run Screen'\",\n   \"storage\":\"Weigh Engine and partially on the HMI; changeable only at factory level via Ethernet\"\n }},\n {\"role\":\"remote_control_cabinet\",\"type_designations\":[],\"characteristics\":{\"note\":\"VersaWeigh and Versa GP may be fitted with remote cabinets provided the load cell is connected to the cabinet using a metal conduit; houses the electrical hardware including the control and display unit (HMI)\"}}\n]\n\nHmm type_designations empty array — omit? Schema: type_designations[]. I'll omit the field or use []. I'll omit for remote cabinet. Also characteristics in components — free-form {} fine.\n\nmatrix_tables: 3 tables as decided.\n\ncertified_type.description: \"Automatic catchweighing instruments (checkweighers): VersaWeigh, Versa GP and Versa Rx constructions (Versa RxC with standard weighing system; Versa RxM and Versa Chain with Slack Conveyor weighing system), constructed in stainless steel as part of an integrated self-carrying cabinet on adjustable feet with see-through door; and Teorema with conveyor on floor-standing steel frame and weigh unit on steel block, with remote electrical cabinet.\"\n\nCategory: \"Checkweigher\".\n\ncertificate: {\"number\":\"R51/2006-GB1-09.04\",\"page_total\":7}\n\nrecommendation: {\"id\":\"R51\",\"edition\":2006,\"accuracy_classes\":[\"XIII\"]} — scheme? The cert scheme (A|B) unknown → omit.\n\nRevision \"0\" — first issuance revision label; history table has no \"revision 0\" row; the first row is the original issue. I'll use revision \"0\" with changes \"Certificate first issued.\" Reasonable.\n\nDouble-check JSON validity: avoid special characters issues — ≤, ≥, µ, ° fine in JSON. Avoid LaTeX. Use \"µV/e\" (micro sign). OK.\n\nOne more consideration: \"Scale interval (e =)\" — canonical \"scale_interval\". Good.\n\nAlso n value 3000 dimensionless unit null.\n\nWrite final JSON.",
